you  we've got stone tools in Britain far  earlier than we thought and in an  environment where we didn't think early  humans were able to live 900 thousand  years ago so it really increases our  understanding of their capabilities at  this time  at the moment sitting of course by the  coast but 800,000 years ago the  landscape would have looked very  different  we'd have been sitting in an early  course of the River Thames in its s3 and  the actual coast would have been about  15 miles out that way the river valley  itself would have been nice and grassy  and open but would have been surrounded  by pine forest and if we carried on  walking down the river to the coast and  carried on walking around the edge of  the coast at this time we were joined to  Europe and we'd end up somewhere like  Amsterdam  the earliest humans evolved in Africa  and they only reached Europe we think  now about 1.6 1.7 million years ago but  they're really limited to the southern  fringes of Europe at this time and until  recently we thought humans didn't reach  Northern Europe - about seven hundred  thousand years ago so we have people at  seven hundred thousand years at Pike  field in Suffolk and those people were  living under Mediterranean conditions  hot dry summers warm wet winters we  thought they were able to come up  briefly in those very warm times and  that's the only time they could get up  here across a land bridge that existed  but now with the new Hayes borough data  we have evidence that even earlier than  that they were actually here under  cooler conditions conditions which were  in some ways comparable with conditions  we might find in say southern  scandinavia at the present day quite  demanding conditions for early humans  and yet they were here in britain  surviving under those conditions the  remarkable thing about Haseman the  reason that we can find this rich  evidence is that as the cliffs erode  back they reveal these river sediments  and these sediments contain remarkable  things like wood animal bones tiny  little things as small as beetle remains  and most importantly of all we have the  evidence of humans in the stone tools  that they left behind  this is one example of the type of flint  tools that were discovering at the site  and in fact we've only found 60 of these  over the last five years work so they're  remarkably rare the important thing is  that they indicate that humans were here  and this particular piece we nicknamed  the butter knife because it's so finely  made and you've got this beautiful  retouch down that edge and in fact this  one piece we sent to the Natural History  Museum  Ct is basically a method for digitally  sectioning an object we take the stone  tool and then using x-rays collect a  series of cross-sectional slices through  the specimen and then stack those slices  up to make a reconstruction of the  specimen but digitally and on a computer  as far as we know this is the first time  anybody's used computed tomography to  analyze stone tools and produce 3d  models and hopefully we can be the first  people to start sharing the data and  really help try and convince people that  what they're looking at is a human may  tool and not just a natural artifact  what Rich's got with this technology is  a way of capturing the shape of these  pieces this flake has been struck off  and we can see here the so-called bulb  of percussion so there was a blow that  detached this flake that was made  purposefully in that position and then  there's been further modification along  the edges by further refined workmanship  so nature might make one or two blows  like this in a river gravel but the  systematic flaking we see on a piece  like that is only done by humans we can  see that it sits very nicely in the hand  and it's got a cutting edge on both  sides one edge a sharper ones more a  blunted scraping surface but this would  have been useful for skinning and  butchering animals getting the meat off  the bones  so we've seen the stone tools that Chris  and Richie were looking at and we  understand how the tools were made and  possibly even used but here on site  we're looking at the context these  gravels were laid down by the ancestral  Thames and Knight has just uncovered a  fragment of woods which is the trunk and  the roots of a small tree and it's  important because we can identify the  plants and the animals that were in the  landscape associated in humans so it  provides a huge amount of information  about the climate's environment in the  landscape at that time so this is just a  range of some of the vertebrate fossils  from the sites this is a tooth of  primitive mammoths these animals showed  that the environment was very rich so  you have a large herbivore fauna but  also the carnivores that were eating  these animals and presumably humans were  also eating some of the large mammals  we've also got aquatic animals this one  here is stunning beautifully preserved  jaw of an extinct semi-aquatic rodent  and you can see the teeth are still in  place in the jaw so again that's another  animal that indicates quite open  environments slow flowing rivers or  ponds large lakes we've also got plant  fossils and because the sediments are  waterlogged and deposited very quickly  the preservation is is truly remarkable  this is a pinecone and it is close to a  million years old but it's it's still  recognizable as a pinecone and from all  of these finds we can build up a very  detailed picture of the environments  from the plant fossils the pollen we  know the vegetation was mostly  coniferous so if you're looking at a  modern-day analogue you would go to the  boreal Zone in in southern Scandinavia  with a mixture of deciduous but dominant  coniferous trees so they're living near  boreal forests and they're surviving  winters that are colder than the present  day and the summers were warm and  reasonable but the winters were cold and  to get through those cold winters they  must have had significant behavioral  adaptations to  with those conditions so it takes us up  a notch in our understanding and  appreciation of what these early people  were capable of and of course there's a  paleontologist I'm hoping we're going to  find some human fossils to go with these  stone tools to really show what these  early people look like  you

